<h2>Channel allocation framework</h2>
<p>For the annual lead target, allocate roughly:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Organic search:</strong> 40-50% (compounds, lowest CAC)</li>
  <li><strong>Referral / partner:</strong> 25-35% (highest close rate)</li>
  <li><strong>Cold outreach:</strong> 15-20% (most consistent, modest CAC)</li>
  <li><strong>Local network:</strong> 10-15% (long-cycle, high quality)</li>
</ul>
<p>I do not run paid acquisition. Every percentage point above is earned through systems I own end to end &mdash; the content, the relationships, the outreach cadence &mdash; not bought.</p>
<p>If any single channel is more than 50% of leads, that is concentration risk, rebalance.</p>

<h2>Capacity ceiling planning</h2>

<p>The hardest part of a sole-prop agency, and the place a 4-hour-a-day discipline forces an honest number rather than a hopeful one.</p>

<p><strong>The weekly hour budget.</strong> 4 hours/day &times; 5 days = 20 hours weekday. Plus a ~1-hour Sunday social-content batch. Total: <strong>21 hours/week, 84 hours over a 4-week month.</strong> See <a href="/sop/calendar/">Calendar &amp; cadence</a> for the day-by-day shape.</p>

<p><strong>What the 84 monthly hours go to (steady state).</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>~8 hours/week active client work (Tue + Thu deep blocks + carry-over slots) = <strong>~32 hr/month</strong></li>
  <li>~3 hours/week marketing &amp; outreach (Mon batch, Wed content slot, alt-week newsletter, Sun social batch) = <strong>~12 hr/month</strong></li>
  <li>~2 hours/week maintenance across all monthly client sites = <strong>~8 hr/month</strong></li>
  <li>~2 hours/week admin (Friday: invoicing, comms, planning) = <strong>~8 hr/month</strong></li>
  <li>~5 hours/week daily overhead (5 &times; 30 min triage + 5 &times; 30 min comms/shutdown) = <strong>~20 hr/month</strong></li>
</ul>
<p>That's ~80 hours, leaving ~4 hours of monthly slack for quarterly QBRs, the occasional emergency, and one-off planning sessions. The whole stack fits inside the 21-hour weekly budget; the moment it doesn't, something has to come out, not the workday extend.</p>

<p><strong>Per-active-client cost.</strong> Every active monthly client requires:</p>
<ul>
  <li>~1 hour/month of content updates and maintenance (most months less; spikes occasionally)</li>
  <li>~30-45 minutes/month of communication / responsiveness overhead</li>
  <li>Quarterly: 30 min QBR (averages to ~10 min/month)</li>
</ul>
<p>So a steady-state monthly client takes <strong>~2 hours/month</strong>, give or take 30 minutes by season. Out of the ~40 hours/month I can dedicate to active client work + maintenance combined (32 + 8), the ceiling is roughly <strong>18-20 active monthly clients</strong> before quality degrades. That's the real number for a 4-hour-a-day operation, and it is materially smaller than what an 8-hour-a-day version of the same agency could carry.</p>

<p><strong>If approaching ceiling.</strong> Three paths, not two:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Close the door on new monthly intake</strong> and let attrition keep the roster steady. Shift new inbound toward lump-sum builds with no monthly maintenance.</li>
  <li><strong>Raise prices.</strong> A 4-hour-a-day agency that is full at 20 monthly clients can charge meaningfully more per client than one with extra capacity to fill. The math works.</li>
  <li><strong>Add capacity via a contractor</strong> (developer or content writer) for specific recurring tasks &mdash; e.g., monthly maintenance pass &mdash; which buys back a few hours/month without extending the day.</li>
</ol>
<p>The path I never take: extending the workday. The 4-hour ceiling is the constraint that protects everything else; the moment it slips, the rest of the system slips with it.</p>

<h2>Annual planning agenda (one-day session)</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Review last year's actuals against last year's plan. What was wrong?</li>
  <li>Set the five questions (above) for the new year.</li>
  <li>Build the channel-allocation table.</li>
  <li>Build the agency-roadmap list (pages, SOPs, samples).</li>
  <li>Build the KPI dashboard with monthly checkpoints.</li>
  <li>Schedule the four quarterly recalibration days on the calendar.</li>
  <li>Schedule the personal-life calendar (family, vacation, no-meeting weeks).</li>
</ol>

<h2>Quarterly recalibration agenda (half-day)</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Pull the YTD numbers against the plan.</li>
  <li>What is on track / off track?</li>
  <li>What needs to change in Q[next]?</li>
  <li>Is the channel mix shifting in a way that needs response?</li>
  <li>Is capacity creeping toward the ceiling?</li>
  <li>What SOPs need an update based on what I learned this quarter?</li>
</ol>

<h2>Common issues</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Goals are not numeric.</strong> "Grow the business" is not a goal. "Reach 25 active monthly clients by Dec 31" is.</li>
  <li><strong>Too many roadmap items.</strong> 4-6 agency improvements per quarter, max. The rest is fantasy and creates false guilt.</li>
  <li><strong>Quarterly recalibration skipped.</strong> Without it, the annual plan ages out by Q2 and becomes irrelevant.</li>
</ul>
